e! I’m pretty sure of this. The highest possible is if someone has a straight of 9s, 10s, Jacks, and Queens - but no Jacks. So no one can beat my straight!\n\nHere’s the plan: Play `33-44-5\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I have a very strong hand structure with a 4-pair straight (33-44-55-66) and high cards (QQ, K, A, 2 is gone). My teammate has only 2 cards left and passed on a single 8, strongly implying they hold a pair (likely saving it for the win). The 4-pair straight is mathematically unbeatable because the Jack bomb was played (removing Jacks) and the 8s are split (I have a pair, others played singles), making it impossible for opponents to form a 4-pair sequence (which requires bridging 8 or J). Playing this sequence clears 8 cards, keeps the initiative in my hand, and allows me to follow up with a pair (QQ or 88) to accommodate my teammate\'s likely hand.\n\nReason: The pair straight 33-44-55-66 is unbeatable (no higher 4-pair sequence exists) and allows me to clear half my hand while retaining control (Free Play). Since my teammate has 2 cards and passed on a single 8, they likely need a pair to go out. By keeping control, I can lead a pair immediately after this play to maximize my teammate\'s winning chances.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥3", "♦3", "♥4", "♦4", "♠5", "♦5", "♣6", "♦6"]}'}
